  "drawer": {
    "_note": "Lower panel, the item rail. Its top edge is the shell's bottom edge offset outward by the same `gap` the dock uses, so the three pieces read as one column with one seam width throughout. The shell's bottom edge is flat, so unlike the dock's underside this edge needs no notch -- there is no raised ear or pod to seat into. Left and right are aligned with the shell's and the dock's.",
    "gap": 13,
    "left": 23,
    "right": 1648,
    "top": 733,
    "bottom": 823,
    "_bottom": "823, not 897. The canvas is cover-scaled (k = max(vw/1672, vh/941)), so the wider the display the more of the canvas's height is cropped: a 21:9 viewport can only see down to y=823 (2560x1080 -> k 1.531 -> 117.8 units cropped top and bottom). 897 would lose 45% of the drawer on those displays. 90 units is the tallest drawer every aspect ratio can show, and it is exactly one row of 66-unit slots plus padding.",
    "r_top": 16,
    "r_bot": 30,
    "_radii": "Top corners take the dock's bottom-corner radius and the bottom corners the dock's top-corner radius, so the column's two outer ends match and the two seam-facing ends match. The offset rule (convex +gap, concave -gap, centres unchanged) is not applied at the corners for the same reason the dock does not apply it either: the left and right edges are aligned with the shell's, so the corner treatment is the piece's own. The seam is therefore 13 across the straight span and slightly wider at the corners, exactly as it is between the dock and the shell.",
